-- title:
--   Toric quotient tower is of multiplicative type
-- statement:
--   The setting is the one of the Néron object for $J_H$ at $p$. Fixed are a prime $p$, a modulus $M$ with $p \mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM2`), and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit that becomes $1$ under the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ (`hHp`). Further fixed are a valuation subring $P\ell$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $P\ell$ (`hPl`, the predicate `LiesOverPrime`), whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$; the hypothesis `hj` that the $q$-expansion `jqModC` of $j$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`; an integral model $\mathfrak{X}$ of type [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`](def/ModularCurve_XHDRModelAtP.html#L81) for the two-chart models of the modular curves of level `ΓM M H` and `XHDRLevel.ΓN p M H hpM` over the ring $R_p =$ `ratLocalizedAt p` of rationals with denominator prime to $p$; level data $\Lambda$ of type `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl`; and a Néron object $O$ of type `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, that is, a smooth separated surjective group scheme $O.g : O.G \to$ `base p` with relative group law $O.L$, a dictionary $O.\mathrm{pts}$ between `JH M H` and the sections over the generic point, Hecke correspondences, and the numerical invariant $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$.
--
--   Two representability hypotheses are imposed. The hypothesis `hrep` asserts that the designation consisting of $O.G$, $O.g$ and the zero section supplied by $O.L.\mathrm{one}$ represents the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ subfunctor of the curve `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ which is cut out by the condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero of the rigidified line bundle over every algebraically closed field valued point): there is a Poincaré rigidified line bundle satisfying the condition, it is universal for rigidified line bundles satisfying the condition, and its restriction along the zero section is trivial. The hypothesis `hrepΛ` asserts the same for the curve of level `XHDRLevel.ΓN p M H hpM`, rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ composed with $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$, and for the designation given by $\Lambda.X$, $\Lambda.f$ and the zero section of $\Lambda.L$.
--
--   Let $R_D$ denote the intersection of $P\ell$ with the fixed field of the decomposition subgroup of $P\ell$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, assumed to be a discrete valuation ring. Over $R_D$ a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}^D$ of height $h$ is given, i.e. a family of finite free cocommutative Hopf $R_D$-algebras $\mathcal{G}^D.\mathrm{level}\,v$ with surjective bialgebra transitions whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals and with $\mathrm{rank}_{R_D}\,\mathcal{G}^D.\mathrm{level}\,v = p^{vh}$. Its points are matched with points of the Jacobian by an additive map $\Delta^D : \mathcal{G}^D.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}) \to$ `JH M H` subject to: injectivity (`hΔDinj`); for every $v$, the image of the level-$v$ points under $\Delta^D$ is exactly $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$, the subgroup generated by the $p^v$-torsion classes whose section extends to the place (`hΔDlev`); Galois equivariance, in the form that for $\tau \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and an $R_D$-algebra automorphism $\tau'$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ agreeing with $\tau$ pointwise one has $\Delta^D(\tau' \cdot z) = \tau \cdot \Delta^D z$ (`hΔDgal`); and the containment $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v) \le O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ for all $v$ (`htorD`), where $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(m)$ is the subgroup generated by the range of $O.\mathrm{toricPoint}\,m$ for $m > 0$.
--
--   The scheme-theoretic incarnation of $\mathcal{G}^D$ inside $O.G$ is given by a ring homomorphism $\rho^D : R_p \to R_D$ and morphisms $\iota^D_v : \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{G}^D.\mathrm{level}\,v \to O.G$, subject to the following group of hypotheses: $\rho^D$ is compatible with the embeddings into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρD`); each $\iota^D_v$ lies over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho^D$ via the structure map of $\mathcal{G}^D.\mathrm{level}\,v$ (`hιDbase`); the induced morphism to the fibre product of $O.g$ with $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho^D$ is a closed immersion (`hιDcl`); each $\iota^D_v$ is annihilated by $p^v$ for the group law, i.e. $\iota^D_v$ followed by $O.L.\mathrm{schemeNsmul}(p^v)$ factors through the unit section (`hιDp`); $\iota^D_v$ is multiplicative, in the sense that for every $R_D$-algebra $B$ and every pair of $B$-points $x,y$ of level $v$ whose associated morphisms lie over the base (hypotheses `hx`, `hy`), the morphism attached to $x y$ is the product of those attached to $x$ and $y$ under $O.L.\mathrm{mul}$ (`hιDmul`); the $\iota^D_v$ are compatible with the transitions of $\mathcal{G}^D$ (`hιDt`); and, for each $v$, writing $j_v$ for the canonical morphism from $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal{G}^D.\mathrm{level}\,v$ into the fibre product over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho^D$ of the kernel of multiplication by $p^v$ (the pullback of $O.L.\mathrm{schemeNsmul}(p^v)$ against the unit section, composed with $O.g$), built from the annihilation identity `h3` and the base compatibility `h4`, the morphism $j_v$ is simultaneously an open immersion and a closed immersion, and every point of that fibre product whose image in $\mathrm{Spec}\,R_D$ is the closed point lies in the range of $j_v$ (`hιDfin`). Finally `hιDpts` requires that for a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $x$ of level $v$ the section of $O$ attached to $\Delta^D$ of the corresponding element of $\mathcal{G}^D.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ is $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the algebra homomorphism of $x$ followed by $\iota^D_v$.
--
--   An $R_D$-algebra structure on $\mathbb{Z}/p$ is fixed, with `hresD` requiring that its structure map have kernel exactly the maximal ideal of $R_D$, so that it realises the residue map. The toric quotient data consist of a family $C$ of commutative rings, each a finite free cocommutative Hopf $R_D$-algebra, together with bialgebra homomorphisms $\pi_v : \mathcal{G}^D.\mathrm{level}\,v \to C_v$ over $R_D$ which are surjective (`hπ`) and cut out the toric classes (`hπpts`): for every $v$ and every $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $x$ of level $v$, the algebra homomorphism of $x$ factors through $\pi_v$ if and only if $\Delta^D$ of the corresponding point lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is the conjunction of three assertions.
--
--   First, for every $v$ there is a bialgebra automorphism $\mathrm{Ver}$ of $\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_D} C_v$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ such that the induced map on Cartier duals, [`CartierDual.map`](def/HopfAlgebra_CartierDualMap.html#L102) applied to $\mathrm{Ver}$, sends every $\chi \in \mathrm{CartierDual}(\mathbb{Z}/p, \mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_D} C_v)$ to $\chi^p$.
--
--   Second, for every $v$, every $R_D$-algebra homomorphism $\chi : C_v \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and every $c \in C_v$, the valuation attached to $P\ell$ of $\chi(c) - \varepsilon(c)$ is $< 1$, where $\varepsilon$ is the counit of $C_v$ followed by the structure map $R_D \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; that is, every $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $C_v$ agrees with the identity section modulo the maximal ideal of $P\ell$.
--
--   Third, there exists a family of $R_D$-bialgebra homomorphisms $t^C_v : C_{v+1} \to C_v$ such that each $t^C_v$ is surjective, $t^C_v \circ \pi_{v+1} = \pi_v \circ \mathcal{G}^D.\mathrm{transition}\,v$, the kernel of $t^C_v$ equals the $p^v$-torsion ideal [`PDivisibleGroup.Hopf.torsionIdeal`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L157) of $C_{v+1}$, and $\mathrm{rank}_{R_D}\,C_v = p^{\,v \cdot O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$ for every $v$.
--
--   This is the multiplicativity statement for the closure of the toric points inside the finite part of the $p$-power torsion of the Néron object over the decomposition ring: the quotient Hopf algebras $C_v$ are of multiplicative type, their $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points reduce to the identity, and they assemble into a $p$-divisible tower of height $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_pDivisibleGroup_toricQuotient_decompositionRing_finitePart_jHNeronObjectAtP`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_pDivisibleGroup_toricQuotient_decompositionRing_finitePart_jHNeronObjectAtP), which packages the family $C$ as a $p$-divisible group, in the analysis of the local behaviour at $p$ of the Galois representation attached to $J_H$.
